countries have constitutions and  sometimes those constitutions aren't  able to deal with certain unprecedented  events like in 1861 when some u.s states  opted to secede and make their own  america or in belgium in 1990 when the  king refused to assent to a new law and  so had to be temporarily declared  incapable of ruling in order to get  around him and pass the law anyway and  australia not wanting to be left out had  its own constitutional crisis in 1975  which saw its prime minister abruptly  fired and replaced by his opponent all  of which came alongside accusations of  cia and british royal involvement but  was this the case and how was it  resolved so first a bit of background as  of 1975 australia wasn't a 100 fully  independent nation state the british  parliament had limited authority to  legislate on behalf of australia and  queen elizabeth ii was australia's head  of state 2 and had constitutional  authority there of course she wasn't  able to personally attend all of the  country she ruled and so in her stead  sat the governor-general and the  governor-general in essence acted as the  prime minister's sort of boss and was  responsible for rubber-stamping any  legislation and calling elections anyway  from 1949 to 1972 australia had been  governed by a coalition of conservative  parties this was until they were  defeated by the labour party and its  leader goff whitlam became the prime  minister now becoming prime minister  depended on controlling australia's  house of representatives but not its  senate the balance of which sat on a  knife edge but the senate also had to  approve legislation for it to be passed  now whitlam had been elected on a  manifesto with increased spending  withdrawing from the vietnam war and  centralizing power which did not sit  well with the conservatives in the  senate whitlam also wished to  investigate the australian security  intelligence organization since it had  previously spied on his party he had  them raided in 1973 and demanded a list  of all cia operatives in australia and  soon after this he publicly accused  numerous people in positions of power of  being american spies as well and the  americans did not respond well to this  and made it clear that if whitlam  continued down this road the us would be  forced to make adjustments after this  things got much better for whitlam and  by better i mean worse because recession  whitlam called for an election in 1974  and whilst he won the house he lost the  senate meaning more deadlock and no more  spending not to worry though because  foreigners exist and whitlam had the  authority to borrow money through  australia's loan council without the  senate's approval whitlam's government  didn't tell the loans council though and  secretly tried to borrow money from  saudi arabia this was leaked and so the  politicians involved did something  clever they lied and when these lies  became public they were promptly sacked  and whitlam's reputation was damaged and  to further add insult to injury a labour  party senator resigned and was replaced  against convention by a conservative  meaning for whitlam he had zero chance  of getting anything through the senate  anymore despite this though whitlam  refused to resign or give his consent to  new elections which the government  general needed to call them and this is  where the crisis begins in earnest the  governor-general of australia at this  time was a man called john kerr a close  friend of whitlam's who was deeply  concerned about the deadlocking  government and so kur came up with a  brilliant solution he fired whitlam and  appointed the leader of the opposition  malcolm fraser as prime minister instead  the problem with this was that at the  time it wasn't exactly clear whether or  not kerr had the authority to do this  kerr had been in contact with buckingham  palace to discuss what he was and wasn't  able to do and those at the palace  reassured him that he had the authority  to dismiss the prime minister and that  if he wanted to do so he would need to  do so quickly because with a single  letter whitlam could have ker removed by  the queen the problem with this is that  in australian politics the queen is  supposed to be neutral in all affairs  and by nudging her in one direction she  would have breached that precedent which  is the issue with this entire crisis  legally everything was above board but  in terms of established precedent which  laid the foundation of trust in the  australian political system everything  was thrown out the window irrespective  of any of this whitlam was removed and  those who supported the labour party  were furious and whitlam was quick to  point out cursed historic ties with the  cia and the intelligence community in  general whereas the conservatives were  happy that the government deadlock  seemed to be coming to an end and in the  election that followed the crisis the  conservative coalition won a resounding  victory and with control of both houses  the crisis was over i hope you enjoyed  this episode and thank you for watching  with a special thanks to my patrons  james bissonette kelly moneymaker mr  wolf sky chappelle jerry lamdin yn  hockey marvin castle jordan longley  gareth turner rod d martin bugley wiggly  captain cydog spencer lightfoot corso  wolfe gustav swan matthew shipley  winston caywood robert wetzel aaron the  white marcus asner maggie paxkowski  anthony beckett alex schwinn mo the  mukwapper coppertone spinning three  plates ben evanson charles the first  adam stalter and scottish trekkie
 

